    <meta name="description" content="What is electromagnetic force?" />
    
</svelte:head>
<article>
    <h1>{$selectedSection}</h1>
    <p>Just like mass, some particles has another property called <em>charge</em>. Charges have two types, <em>positive</em> and <em>negative</em>. Like charges <em>repel</em> and unlike charges <em>attract</em>. Compared to gravitation, electromagnetic force is much stronger. <em>Electrons</em> have negative charge and <em>protons</em> has positive charge. Atoms are electrically neutral. An overall charge can appear of an object have unequal number of electrons and protons.</p>
    <section>
        <h2>Coulomb's law</h2>
        <p>Just like expression for force due to gravitation, there is an expression for force between two charged particles, known as the <em>Coulomb's law</em>. Suppose two charged particles with charges {`\\(Q\\)`} and {`\\(q\\)`} are placed at separation {`\\(r\\)`}, then the magnitude of force between them is given as {`\\[F=\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0}\\frac{qQ}{r^2}\\]`} where {`\\(\\epsilon_0=8.85419\\times 10^{-12}C^2/Nm^2\\)`}.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Contact force</h2>
        <p>
            When two objects come in contact with each other, their atoms come close enough to feel electromagnetic repulsion. This is known as the <em>contact force</em>.
        </p>
